<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- v1.0: the privacy manifest. Required since May 2024; an app that touches
	     a required-reason API without declaring it here is rejected (ITMS-91053).

	     This file is a RESOURCE, not a source, and it sits inside SalarySeed/
	     for exactly one reason: the folder-synchronized group copies everything
	     under that directory into the bundle, and a privacy manifest that is not
	     in the bundle is not a privacy manifest. It is the only non-Swift,
	     non-asset file allowed there, and tools/check_privacy_manifest.sh fails
	     the build if it stops arriving. Nothing in the project file names it, so
	     nothing in the project file would otherwise notice. -->

	<!-- No tracking, in Apple's sense: nothing here is ever linked to data from
	     other companies' apps or sites, and there is no ad network, no analytics
	     SDK and no third-party framework of any kind in this app. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- UserDefaults. SalaryStore persists the salary and the profile there
		     and SupporterStore mirrors the entitlement. All of it is this app
		     reading and writing its own defaults, which is what CA92.1 covers.
		     Nothing reads another app's defaults or an app group. -->
		<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
		<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
		<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
		<array>
			<string>CA92.1</string>
		</array>
	</array>

	<!-- NOTHING IS COLLECTED, and unlike most apps that say so, there is no code
	     path here that could. The app has no networking of its own: no URLSession,
	     no endpoint, no analytics. The only traffic it generates is StoreKit
	     talking to Apple around the support sheet, and that is between the device
	     and Apple, carries no app data, and is not collection by us.

	     The Keychain is not used. The salary, the profile answers and the
	     preferences live in this app's own UserDefaults and go when the app is
	     deleted.

	     v1.1 added the payslip checker, and this array did NOT change. A payslip
	     is read into memory, checked and discarded: it is never written to disk,
	     never uploaded, and no figure from it is stored next to the salary or
	     the profile. PDFKit, Vision, PhotosPicker and the document picker are
	     none of them required-reason APIs, and nothing in that feature reads a
	     file timestamp or the disk's free space, so NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes
	     above did not change either. If a payslip history is ever added, both of
	     those stop being true in the same commit, and so do the App Privacy
	     answers in App Store Connect.

	     v1.2 made the checker a tab, and this array did not change either. What
	     changed is only how long the reading lives in MEMORY: it used to die
	     with the cover, and it now survives a trip to another tab, until the
	     reader checks another payslip or quits the app. Nothing about that
	     reaches storage, which is the only thing this file is about. The
	     sentence that has to stay true is "never written to disk", and it is.

	     A contribution pool was built and tested and is NOT in this build. It
	     lives on the pool-backend branch. If it ever ships, this array stops
	     being correct and App Store Connect's App Privacy answers change in the
	     same submission, which is why that work stayed on a branch rather than
	     behind a flag. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
